I just lost a great game... no, really...

I had completed my quest,

Spoilers ---

I.e. got the awesome Tsurugi of Muramasa (The razorsharp blade cuts the
woodland elf in half!) and enchanted it to +3 (discarding my +4 Excalibur),
had the Bell of Opening (whatever that is good for),
had gray dragon scale mail, an AC of -24, resistance to all but disintegration,
am. of reflection & lifesaving, speed boots, teleport control...
had identified several valuable gems, had a luck stone and max luck,
max alignment...
Oh yes I had raided Fort Ludios...

This was my first game to have even SEEN the quest stuff.
I didn't cheat, except that I read a few rumorish spoilers in this newsgroup
(like how to get out of the quest!)

So what happened?  I reached what I guess was the Medusa level, with
lots of water.  Both my wands of cold turned out empty, and I didn't feel
like searching up a zillion levels for my jumping boots, so I dug through
the ground and came out on the castle level.  So far so good.
I went to the drawbridge and rang the Bell of Opening.  No dice.
I had a spell of knocking (oh yeah, I had quite a few neat spells) so
I used it to open the drawbridge.  Good enough.  I go to the open door
and start whacking monsters.  whackwhackwhackwhackwhack...
there's a soldier zapping a wand of striking at me but who cares...
whackwhackwhack... then boom:
  The soldier zaps a wand of striking!
  The wand misses the soldier.
  The wand hits the soldier!
  The drawbridge collapses into the moat!
  You are blown apart by flying debris.
  Do you want your possessions identified?

Say what????
I wasn't even standing on the drawbridge!  Well to be clear, I was standing
here:
  .....}|@LTER...
  .....}|@...O...
me -->..@@@.@....
  .....}|.MT.....
  .....}|ENLO@...

in the open space left by the drawbridge.

Well, I guess that's the way the drawbridge crumbles.
Just be forewarned.  Don't stand there while fighting scads of monsters.
And beware lest the drawbridge break and you unwittingly walk into the moat.

I just looked at the code for 3.0 (I'm trying not to look at the code for 3.1),
and it seems to me there's an oversimplification in the destroy_drawbridge()
routine.  There are two squares that are considered drawbridge territory.
Apparently any occupants of either square are killed regardless of whether
the drawbridge was up or down.  Doesn't seem right...  ;-(

Well, time to try again ... This Time For Sure!!

By the way, the Samurai Quest is great stuff!
I'm sure the others will be too!
...although the Samurai quest relied heavily on Japanese culture &
history, and I can't see how that would be applied to some of the
other classes.
I can't wait to see.

And have you tried the teleport-control shortcuts?
shift-<dir> moves you in big steps; <, >, _, and maybe some others
move you to the next stairs/altar/etc.  Excellent ideas.

Another thing I liked was when I was levitating, and I fired
an arrow (ya) to the left, and I floated to the right.  Cute, cute.

Then there were the angelic beings... so far I've seen coatls and
Aleaxes, but they've always been peaceful, so I don't know what they're
really like yet. :-)
Also the mind flayer or whatever it's called.  I only saw one once:
I came into a room, thought it was a hobbit, killed it in one stroke,
then realized it was something else; I wish I had held off a bit
to see what it does.  Well, I'm sure I'll see it again some time.


Fort Ludios was fun.
Discovered it totally by accident.
Fortunately my Samurai had really good auto-search... I don't know
why...
Anyway, lots of gray dragons, and a big dance hall :-)
(I won't spoil your fun if you haven't been there.)
Strange thing though, all metal objects left on the level turned
"thoroughly rusty", and spellbooks turned "plain".  High humidity??


Happy hacking,

